  "worked_solution": "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-4{x}^{2}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}} & =1+(-4{x}^{2})+{(-4{x}^{2})}^{2}+{(-4{x}^{2})}^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-4{x}^{2}+16{x}^{4}-64{x}^{6}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get an expansion for $\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}},$ we multiply the above expansion by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{1+4{x}^{2}} & =2(1-4{x}^{2}+16{x}^{4}-64{x}^{6}+\u22ef) \\\\  & =2-8{x}^{2}+32{x}^{4}-128{x}^{6}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
